<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" target="china_maritime_pressure::export_control_stringenc" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising regulatory scrutiny and expected enforcement around AI technologies raises political and bureaucratic pressure to broaden and tighten outbound export controls on hardware, software and know‑how transfers.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">china_maritime_pressure</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" target="global_monetary_stress::intervention_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Intensified regulatory actions (export controls, licensing blocks, sanctions) create abrupt market and payment frictions for targeted sectors/countries, provoking more frequent and larger public-sector FX and liquidity interventions to stabilize markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" target="hormuz_standoff::geo_tension_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Tighter export controls and enforcement on AI-related technology provoke reciprocal restrictions, public accusations and targeted sanctions between the US and China that spill over into harder rhetoric and posturing by regional actors around Hormuz.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" target="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Tighter export controls and regulatory enforcement on AI models, chips and tooling reduce the flow of high-end autonomy components and software to Russian procurement networks, degrading the tempo and effectiveness of newly fielded UAVs and lowering strike frequency over ensuing deployment cycles.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_perception" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Public regulatory measures, sanctions and export restrictions by major powers signal technological decoupling and raise expectations among regional actors and markets that support lines and escalation-containment mechanisms may deteriorate, increasing perceived likelihood of broader conflict spillover.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
