<edge source="china_maritime_pressure::militarization_level" target="global_monetary_stress::safe_haven_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising maritime militarization raises perceived geopolitical risk and near‑term downside tail risk for regional trade and earnings, prompting portfolio reallocations into safe‑haven assets (USD, JPY, gold) and increasing safe‑haven demand flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_maritime_pressure</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_maritime_pressure::militarization_level" target="hormuz_standoff::geo_tension_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">An uptick in Chinese maritime militarization (more patrols, exercises, and forward-deployed assets) triggers reciprocal deployments and sharper diplomatic warnings from the US and regional partners, directly elevating geopolitical tensions surrounding the Strait of Hormuz.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_maritime_pressure</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_maritime_pressure::militarization_level" target="hormuz_standoff::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher Chinese naval operational tempo and close-approach tactics increase the likelihood of miscommunications and physical encounters between state and commercial vessels, raising the frequency of maritime incidents affecting traffic that transits Hormuz.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_maritime_pressure</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_maritime_pressure::militarization_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_perception" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A marked rise in Chinese maritime posture compels the US and NATO to shift naval and diplomatic attention toward the Indo‑Pacific, which Russian decision‑makers and markets perceive as reducing Western capacity and willingness to escalate militarily in Europe, lowering regional escalation expectations.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_maritime_pressure</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_maritime_pressure::militarization_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising maritime militarization heightens national-security concerns that drive policymakers and regulators to increase scrutiny, investigatory activity, and enforcement risk for AI-related technologies and collaborations tied to the region.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_maritime_pressure</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
